From fcd710d32b40ba4dd6e920364992fdbc5b754ad4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vili Kohonen <vili.kohonen@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 11:41:01 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Log reward metrics through the trainer, so a batch lands on
 one wandb row

detail/rh/* and rewards/* were one wandb row behind the critic/* and response_length/*
metrics computed from the very same batch. The cause is src/wandb_utils.py's wandb_log:
`wandb.log(*args, commit=False)` with no `step`. Called from inside the reward function,
it appends to whichever row is still open -- and the reward runs earlier in iteration N
than verl's `logger.log(data=metrics, step=N)`, so the open row is N-1. Verl then flushes
it and opens N. One batch, two rows.

Measured on the 08-20 baseline over steps 58-74: n_strict_rh from rollouts/N.jsonl matches
wandb at N-1 on 17 of 17 steps, while critic/score/mean from the same file matches at N on
17 of 17.

Three consequences, all fixed by this: the rollout dumps and wandb needed different joins
depending on the metric family; two families on one wandb row described different batches,
with nothing to give it away; and the last step of a completed run carried no detail/rh/*
at all, because the final batch's counters were buffered into the previous row and nothing
followed to open another.

Reward functions now buffer into pending_metrics, and the trainer drains them into the dict
it logs with an explicit step. register_metrics is removed with them: it defined the reward
groups against training/global_step to suppress wandb's out-of-order-step warning, which was
this bug's warning, and keeping it would suggest alignment was handled elsewhere.

Reading older runs: add 1 to the wandb step of any detail/rh/* or rewards/* value to get the
batch it came from. Every arm shifts equally, so no comparison between runs changes.

Not fixed here: src/train/screening.py logs the same way. Screening is not implemented for
verl, so it is dead code today, and touching it would be a change with nothing to test it.

tests/test_reward_metric_alignment.py covers the buffer contract on CPU.
